{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And her father heard her vow, etc., and all her vows shall stand, etc.</b> One needs to know why it began speaking in singular language, as it said ״נִדְרָהּ״ (her vow), and concluded speaking in plural language, as it said ״נְדָרֶיהָ״ (her vows). Also, why does it multiply words, ״כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ״ (all her vows) and ״כָּל אִסָּר״ (every binding oath) — it should have said merely ״וְקָמוּ נְדָרֶיהָ וַאֲסָרֶיהָ״ (and her vows and her bindings shall stand). Further, why did it vary regarding vows, saying <b>וְקָמוּ נְדָרֶיהָ</b> (and her vows shall stand), but regarding bindings, <b>יָקוּם</b> (it shall stand)? Also, it should not have needed to say ״יָקוּם״ and could have relied on the statement ״וְקָמוּ״ that it said at the beginning." "And the verse may be explained based on what they said in tractate Nedarim, folio 87a, and this is the language of the Mishnah: She said, ״Konam figs and grapes to me״ — if he upheld [the vow] regarding the figs, all of it is upheld; if he annulled [it] regarding the figs, it is not annulled until he also annuls [it] regarding the grapes. If she said, ״Konam grapes that I do not taste, and figs that I do not taste״ — these are two vows, end of quote. And they said in the Talmud, and this is their language: Our Mishnah is according to Rabbi Yishmael, as it is taught in a baraita: ״אִישָׁהּ יְקִימֶנּוּ״ (her husband shall uphold it) — she said, ״Konam figs and grapes״ etc.; if he upheld [it] regarding the figs, all of it is upheld etc.; if he annulled [it] regarding the grapes, it is not annulled until he also annuls [it] regarding the figs — these are the words of Rabbi Yishmael. Rabbi Akiva says: Behold, it says ״אִישָׁהּ יְקִימֶנּוּ״ etc. — just as יְקִימֶנּוּ (he upholds it) [can apply] to part of it, so too יְפִירֶנּוּ (he annuls it) [can apply] to part of it. And Rabbi Yishmael: Is it written יָפֵר מִמֶּנּוּ (he annuls part of it)? And Rabbi Akiva: He draws an analogy between annulment and upholding. Rabbi Yochanan said: This is the view of Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva, but the Sages say: They draw an analogy from upholding to annulment — just as annulment [takes effect only on] what he annulled, so too upholding [takes effect only on] what he upheld — end of quote. This is the version (girsa) in the books before us, but the Tosafot and Ramban and the Ran have the version: But the Sages say: Just as annulment — what he annulled is not annulled, so too etc. And according to this version, the Sages and Rabbi Yishmael follow one and the same reasoning regarding annulment that is not annulled, and this version is primary, as they proved from the Tosefta." "<b>We thus find</b> three opinions being stated: (1) This is the opinion of Rabbi Yishmael, which Rabbi [Yehuda HaNasi] brought in the Mishnah anonymously — that if he upheld part of the vow, he upheld all of it, and if he annulled part of the vow, it is not annulled even the part that he annulled. (2) The opinion of Rabbi Akiva — that whether he upheld part or annulled part, it is as though he upheld or annulled all of it. (3) The opinion of the Sages — who hold that whether in annulment or in upholding, his upholding or annulment has no effect at all unless he upheld or annulled all of it. And according to each of the three opinions, the verse will be explained correctly." "According to the opinion of Rabbi Yishmael, whom Rabbi [Yehuda HaNasi] cited anonymously in the Mishnah, it will be explained in this manner: the verse's saying ״וְשָׁמַע אָבִיהָ אֶת נִדְרָהּ״ in singular language — to say that the verse is speaking of a case where she vows a single vow, such as where she said, ״Konam figs and grapes,״ which is called one vow — to exclude if she said, ״Konam figs that I do not taste, konam grapes״ etc., which is called two vows." "And the verse's saying <b>וְהֶחֱרִישׁ</b> (and he kept silent) etc., <b>וְקָמוּ</b> all her vows — meaning: even if he kept silent only regarding one particular item, meaning that regarding the figs and the grapes which she vowed, he kept silent only regarding the figs — ״וְקָמוּ כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ״ — meaning, even regarding the grapes. And from this you are alerted that in the verse's saying <b>וְהֶחֱרִישׁ</b>, the intention is not regarding everything, for behold, it was necessary to inform you regarding the upholding, and it said <b>וְקָמוּ כָּל</b> (all her vows shall stand) — from this you learn that up to this point, in the statement <b>וְהֶחֱרִישׁ</b>, we were not dealing with all her vows." "And the verse's saying <b>וכל אסר</b> etc. <b>יקום</b> — by this change it intended to use the singular form, to reveal that the phrase ״כל נדריה״ does not mean they are many vows, but rather a single vow, except that she included many things in it — figs and grapes; and in the formulation of vows it uses the plural form, as it says ״וכל אסר״ etc. ״יקום״. And although the statement uses the term אסר, the verse has already informed you that the law of a נדר and a שבועה are equal." "And the verse's saying <b>יקום</b> — and it did not rely on the earlier statement ״וקמו״, for if so I would explain the verse's intent to be that if she vowed and swore concerning two things together and he remained silent regarding the נדר, he also ratified the שבועה; therefore it says ״יקום״ to say that ratification is required for each one of them separately." "And the verse's saying <b>ואם הניא</b> etc. <b>כל נדריה</b> — meaning, he must annul all her vows; that is, if he annulled only for the figs it is not annulled until he also annuls for the grapes. And the verse's saying <b>לא יקום</b> in the singular form is to inform you that the verse is speaking of a single vow, lest you err from the phrase ״נדריה״ etc. — as though the verse were speaking only of one who vows many vows, according to the law stated in the latter clause of the mishnah that we cited above (Nedarim 66a)." "<b>And according to the reasoning</b> of Rabbi Akiva, that annulment of part annuls all, and ratification of part likewise ratifies all, it may be explained in this manner: <b>ושמע אביה את נדרה</b> etc. <b>והחריש</b> etc. — meaning, if he heard even part of her vow, even though he did not hear her entire vow, and remained silent regarding that part, by this <b>וקמו כל נדריה</b> — even the part he did not hear. And the verse's saying <b>ואם הניא אביה אותה</b> — meaning, even if he did not annul everything but only one part, which is what we call ״הניא אותה״. And therefore it did not join the phrase ״כל נדריה״ to the phrase ״ואם הניא״ in this manner: ״ואם הניא אביה כל נדריה ביום שמעו״, for then it would not have needed to say the word ״אותה״. And accordingly, the phrase ״כל נדריה״ etc. carries forward below to the phrase ״לא יקום״. And the verse's saying <b>לא יקום</b> in the singular form is intended by this to state the novel ruling that even what he did not annul is annulled." "<b>And according to this</b> approach the verse's statement <b>וה׳ יסלח לה</b> etc. will be properly resolved — meaning, that if her father annulled only for the grapes and she does not know the law that the annulment also took effect for the figs, and she ate figs, the verse informs us that HaShem will forgive her, for she requires forgiveness since as far as she knows her father did not annul regarding the figs. And it gives the reason for the forgiveness: ״כי הניא אביה אותה״ — meaning, for the annulment her father made also took effect for this. And it was necessary to say this to inform you that the matter for which forgiveness comes is on account of this lack of knowledge, from which the unwitting error arose. And understand this." "<b>And according to the reasoning of</b> the Sages who said that annulment does not avail for part and establishment does not avail for part, until he annuls all or establishes all, it is explained along these lines: ״וְשָׁמַע אָבִיהָ אֶת נִדְרָהּ״ — it used singular language to alert you that the verse is speaking of her having made a single vow, and not of her having made two vows, as I explained according to the reasoning of Rabbi Yishmael. And its saying <b>וְהֶחֱרִישׁ</b> etc. <b>וְקָמוּ כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ</b> — the phrase ״כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ״ is drawn forward and still further forward, and this is its meaning: ״וְהֶחֱרִישׁ לָהּ אָבִיהָ״ — when does silence avail to establish? If there are ״כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ״ — but for a part of them, establishment does not avail for him." "And its saying <b>וְאִם הֵנִיא</b> etc. <b>כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ</b> etc. — the phrase ״כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ״ is drawn upward along these lines: <b>וְאִם הֵנִיא</b> etc. — if in his annulment he nullified everything, then no particular item among them is established. And it comes to inform you that if he nullified one particular item, we do not apply to it ״לֹא יָקוּם.״ And although one could say that the phrase ״כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ״ is drawn downward to the phrase ״לֹא יָקוּם,״ it is explained along these lines: ״כָּל נְדָרֶיהָ״ etc. ״לֹא יָקוּם״ — meaning, at a time when all her vows are included as a whole, then the particular item exists. From this you have learned that there is no nullification of a particular item alone. And all this is specifically when she made a single vow, as indicated by the phrase ״וְשָׁמַע אָבִיהָ אֶת נִדְרָהּ״; but if she made them in the manner of two vows, one is not dependent on the other."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {1 "The Or HaChaim quotes the Mishnah and Gemara in Nedarim 87a and reports a variant girsa; the translations of the Talmudic passages follow his Hebrew renderings closely, including the variant reading he attributes to Tosafot, Ramban, and the Ran."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:42.655765256Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}